WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:07.540
Can a tiny scrap of membrane from one cell switch on only a matching target cell?

2
00:00:08.840 --> 00:00:13.660
If that vesicle forms inside the sender, how does its signal end up facing outward?

3
00:00:15.020 --> 00:00:18.060
An immune case and a membrane trick answer both questions.

4
00:00:19.233 --> 00:00:24.773
B cells release tiny membrane sacs called extracellular vesicles into the fluid

5
00:00:24.773 --> 00:00:31.633
around them. These vesicles display peptide-loaded MHC class II molecules on their

6
00:00:31.633 --> 00:00:37.853
outer surface. They switch on only the T cells whose receptor matches that peptide.

7
00:00:39.133 --> 00:00:40.533
Nothing enters the T cell.

8
00:00:41.567 --> 00:00:45.587
Why would a protein on a vesicle made inside the cell point outward?

9
00:00:46.887 --> 00:00:51.587
Follow a receptor that starts on the cell surface, its coral head facing outside.

10
00:00:52.927 --> 00:00:58.287
First, the plasma membrane pinches inward by endocytosis to form an endosome.

11
00:00:59.527 --> 00:01:04.167
That first flip points the head into the endosome, a trapped pocket of outside

12
00:01:04.167 --> 00:01:05.247
fluid. . . .

13
00:01:05.667 --> 00:01:11.307
Next, the endosome membrane bulges into the endosome's own interior, away from the

14
00:01:11.307 --> 00:01:13.287
cytosol, forming small buds.

15
00:01:14.727 --> 00:01:17.467
Each bud fills with a drop of cytosol as it grows.

16
00:01:18.847 --> 00:01:22.567
Before it pinches off, predict where the coral receptor head ends up.

17
00:01:28.450 --> 00:01:33.810
The second flip cancels the first one, so the coral head faces outward again as the

18
00:01:33.810 --> 00:01:37.770
bud pinches off. The cytosol stays sealed inside.

19
00:01:39.170 --> 00:01:45.010
Extracellular vesicles keep cell orientation: ligands face out, internal cargo stays

20
00:01:45.010 --> 00:01:50.330
sealed. A compartment packed with these buds is a multivesicular body.

21
00:01:51.400 --> 00:01:55.640
Picture this multivesicular body as a sorting room with two doors.

22
00:01:56.800 --> 00:02:02.240
If it fuses with a lysosome, digestive enzymes break the internal vesicles down.

23
00:02:03.500 --> 00:02:07.860
If instead it moves to the surface and fuses with the plasma membrane, those

24
00:02:07.860 --> 00:02:09.940
vesicles spill outside intact.

25
00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:13.560
Those released vesicles are exosomes.

26
00:02:14.633 --> 00:02:19.773
Cells also release microvesicles by budding straight outward from the plasma

27
00:02:19.773 --> 00:02:26.193
membrane. That direct step also buds away from the cytosol, giving the exact same

28
00:02:26.193 --> 00:02:33.813
orientation. Because exosomes and microvesicles overlap in size and markers, call

29
00:02:33.813 --> 00:02:37.173
vesicles of unproven origin extracellular vesicles.

30
00:02:38.267 --> 00:02:43.107
This right-side-out layout gives a vesicle three ways to act on a target cell.

31
00:02:44.507 --> 00:02:49.607
First, a surface ligand binds a target receptor directly, like the B cell vesicles.

32
00:02:51.007 --> 00:02:56.387
Second, the vesicle membrane can fuse with a target membrane and spill sealed cargo

33
00:02:56.387 --> 00:02:57.387
into the cytosol.

34
00:02:58.400 --> 00:03:02.620
Third, the target cell can swallow the vesicle by endocytosis.

35
00:03:04.120 --> 00:03:08.860
Swallowing alone is not a signal, because swallowed cargo usually ends up in the

36
00:03:08.860 --> 00:03:15.140
lysosome. Swallowed internal cargo acts only if it escapes or is sensed along the

37
00:03:15.140 --> 00:03:20.460
way. Orientation decides what acts on contact and what stays sealed.
